sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/levin-2022-tame-26> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Ion channel phylogeny is ancient (many channel families likely predate the most recent unicellular ancestor of animals), and the appearance of context-sensitive channels enabling new bioelectric feedback loops tracks the appearance of complex body plans at the emergence of metazoa, indicating an evolutionary continuum from single-cell excitability to cognition via somatic pattern control.";
    dc:type "historical-inference";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Context-sensitive ion channels appear alongside complex metazoan body plans";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Levin M, “Technological Approach to Mind Everywhere: An Experimentally-Grounded Framework for Understanding Diverse Bodies and Minds”, Frontiers in systems neuroscience (2022), doi:10.3389/fnsys.2022.768201; section “Evolutionary Aspects › Somatic Bioelectrics Reveals the Origin of Complex Cognitive Systems”. Scope: Ion channel gene families across unicellular eukaryotes and early-branching metazoans"
}
